every one for those who don't know me  I'm Christopher kettle and I'm chairman  of Stratford District Council and I'm  talking to you on here's another very  sunny afternoon spent on the lockdown  the reason I'm talking to you is on  Friday we should be celebrating beed day  across the district it was not so long  ago a few months the detailed plans are  being made not only in Stratford but in  towns villages and Hamlet's and across  the whole country  to commemorate and celebrate the end of  the Second World War and it is strange  that only two years ago we celebrated  the scenary centenary of the First World  War armistice and now we're  commemorating another armed armistice 27  years after that one what that first  commemoration achieved was both a  realization and education of the  appalling nature of the first war and  the extraordinary bravery and the  actions carried out by soldiers sailors  and airmen in that conflict and the  devastating impact on those left behind  him home in this district we prepared a  roll of Honor of all those the district  who died in that conflict fifteen  hundred and seventy one in total about  one-sixth of the male population aged  between 20 and 40 one impacts with the  current problems has been that we have  not seen or heard from the many veterans  of what it was like to survive the rural  and very modern and highly mechanized  war very different from the trenches and  see in air battles of World War one many  of us though will have had parents of  relatives who served in the Second World  War whether in Europe the Middle East or  the Far East and again on land sea or  air  however what was different was that in  this war civilians were heavily involved  thousands died in the bombing of major  cities and like all unlike all previous  Wars  the Second World War was visible to  those at home  I was recently speaking to a gentleman  who as a child at the time but much of  the war on my farm explained that I was  seeing from the farm a night sky being  lit up over Coventry yet another bombing  range and where his mother was working  as an ambulance driver he was told it  was a special fireworks play to avoid  him worrying about his mother there will  have been many other similar memories  and from those beneath the bombs  many tragic stories for the survivors  the end of the war was a cause for  celebration and it is an enormous pity  that we cannot commemorate the 75th  anniversary as we had all planned  both celebrating and remembering the end  of that war in Europe again in Stratford  we were preparing and we'll prepare a  district role of Honor to commemorate  all those who fell in that war and yet  it is extraordinary that we cannot  commemorate the war because we are now  fighting a different war but one in  which the aggressor is silent invisible  and indiscriminate and with whom that  can be no dialogue or armistice and so  what we are seeing is a very different  army fighting again to save our lives  not only across this country but across  that globe from the front line now  staffed not by soldiers but by doctors  nurses carers the support staff and  those supporting them and also those  trying to ensure that the vulnerable are  not forgotten and that includes every  one of us either those who are still  working in essential roles or those  importantly isolating at home we all  have a part to play  but on Friday let us for a short time  forget about our current battle and  commemorate the broadcast seventy-five  years ago when Sir Winston Churchill  announced the end of the war in Europe  so in the morning there will be two  minutes silence at 11:00 a.m. if you  wish to join in on that and if you're  having a VE Day picnic in your garden  please remember stay safe and distant  but celebrate and remember both to raise  your glass at 3:00 p.m. in celebration  and remember that the Queen will be  addressing the nation at 9:00 let us  commemorate the extraordinary acts of  those in the services and the civilians  and of course those now fighting this  new war today and let us also not forget  for them and their families and it and  is traditional at the end of the speech  like this this is also god save the  queen thank you all for listening and I  hope you have a great day on Friday  thank you bye-bye
 

